hi there Steve Kaufman here today I want  to talk about grammar now  often here at my channel there has been  discussion of you know the importance of  input and the need to de-emphasize  grammar instruction when it comes to  language learning and this very often  causes a great debate between those  people who love grammar and those people  who are averse to learning grammar so I  want to talk a little bit about grammar  and how it relates now to my most recent  language which is Romanian and of course  every language we learn is different for  us because in each case we either know a  related language or don't know a related  language there is common vocabulary with  languages that we know or there isn't  so Romanian obviously is very different  from Korean or Russian or Czech because  in Romanian there's you know whatever  3/4 of the words are more or less  recognizable as being similar to  vocabulary that I already know in other  Romance languages so that makes it very  different from learning Korean Chinese  or Slavic languages the other thing  that's different about Romanian for me  this time is that I want to learn it in  a hurry because I'm gonna be in Romania  for 3 days only and I just want to be  able to communicate while I'm there and  understand as much as possible  normally my strategy is to just sit back  and let it come at me with the intention  of eventually achieving a high level of  comprehension in the language and also  the ability to communicate with you know  people who have common interests I don't  think that in the six weeks that I have  I'm gonna be able to achieve that in  Romanian so and of course when I started  we didn't have a lot of content at link  so I went and got Pimsleur as I've  talked and I explained and then I went  and got teach yourself and I've been  using both of those I also was able to  get ahold of a very good grammar book  for Romanian so I want to talk about how  I use that book and how it relates to  my language learning and what I think is  the ideal way to tackle grammar first of  all I have never said that one should  not look at grammar I have always said  that we look at grammar from time to  time we look at the tables we look at  the rules but we don't expect to learn  it we don't expect to learn the table at  least I don't expect to learn the table  when I look at the table I don't think  it's possible to nail down the basics so  that then they're solid and then you can  just go on and learn words I find that  the grammar sort of drips in bit by bit  things start to become clearer over time  and as part of the puzzle becomes  clearer some other parts then are more  accessible and it's difficult to predict  what's going to be difficult in what's  going to be easy for example you know  present tense past tense  it may not be difficult to learn both  those things at the same time but some  issues relating to pronouns might be  very difficult to grasp for quite a long  time so what I think is the ideal way to  deal with grammar is first of all to  deal with it in conjunction with a lot  of input so that the grammar  explanations are relate to something  that you already have experience with or  because you have seen these structures  and you're now curious so if you're  curious about the whys why does this  happen you're more likely to be  interested and to understand the  explanations if you are hit with these  explanations without enough experience  with the language the explanations kind  of go over your head so the first thing  is you want to vary the input activities  in my opinion with the grammar learning  the second thing about grammar is or the  thing that I dislike the most about any  grammar book or where grammar is  introduced is when they ask you  questions  the assumption is because because they  have taught you something a structure a  pattern some rule that you should be  able to reproduce it that hasn't that is  never my experience all the grammar rule  or the table or the explanation does it  starts to make you aware of some  thing at least that's my experience so  that when I next read something oh yeah  it clicks in oh that's that and so  between reading it and experiencing it  and reading the rule again and then  experiencing it again slowly these  patterns become part of our you know  things that were able to understand and  eventually use so I don't like being  questioned the what and teach yourself  they have a lot of these questions and  drills they have them on the audio and  they have them in the book I never  bother but what is useful is if they  have you know questions relating to a  specific issue with the future tense or  the past tense or whatever it might be  then it is useful to go to the answer  section and read the ten example the ten  answers because the answers will all be  of the same pattern so that helps to  reinforce that pattern and so that's the  next point about a grammar book a  grammar book should be light on  explanation should have tables and  should have lots of examples so even if  a table is introduced which is a good  reference tool then you need to have  lots of examples of how these tenses are  used or how these pronouns are used and  so you read those examples and you  reread these examples and then again  when you encounter other examples of  this pattern in your listing and reading  it all starts to click in so and and I  don't like being questioned because I  don't like being forced to to reproduce  things that's part of the reason why I'm  not normally so keen to speak early  although as I'll explain a little later  in the case of Romanian I am doing that  so I don't like being asked grammar  questions I don't like being asked  comprehension questions about a text  that I've listened to I think both of  these destroy the pleasure of learning  if you ask me to talk about something  that I've read to talk if I'm reading  something on the radio Romania about you  know unemployment in Romania we can talk  about unemployment in Romania but if you  ask me to remember details of the  article and to demonstrate that I  understood it correctly well first of  all I won't remember the details and  I probably didn't understand it  correctly and it doesn't matter because  by reading the article and listening to  audio related to the article I am  getting exposure to the language and  then I with that background I can go and  read my grammar rules and my grammar  examples and it's all this constant  reinforcing circle of input review of  the grammar back to the input and so  forth one other thing I say that I  normally don't like to speak early  because there's no need to there's no  pressure if I'm learning Russian or  Czech and I'm not gonna go there for  another year  then I have no great need to start  speaking early because it's difficult to  arrange I'm not gonna change my social  life just to go out and find you know  Russians or Czechs here in Vancouver to  eat with three times a week I can have  my discussions on Skype which is fine  but then you know that's less portable  to me than just listening while I'm in  the car so that my initial period is  it's not stressful I just read a lesson  on my own time and at some point after  five six months or eight months and I  start speaking but I can't do that with  Romanian because I'm gonna be in Romania  in June and I'll only have three days  there so I've decided I'm gonna start  speaking early like I started on  Romanian two weeks ago I have now had  two discussions with a tutor at Linc and  I've signed up for more and of course  it's a real struggle to say anything but  it again it's it's it's it helps me to  notice where my gaps are I get a report  back from my tutor with about 20 phrases  that I used incorrectly  I save them they go into my flashcards I  review them it's it's it's part of my  learning process and and of course with  Romanian I can do it because even now if  I read you know if I look at what I'm  doing I'm reviewing those phrases that I  had recorded for me some other content  that has now come up come into our  library at Lake but the biggest part of  what I'm doing is I am reading news  articles from Radio Romania and  and listening to the news reports now  they're not transcripts but a lot of the  vocabulary repeats and so my ability to  my ability to understand the written  article is very high because there's so  much common vocabulary my ability to  understand the news report the  discussions or whatever is of course  much much lower but I'm doing a lot of  that to get exposed to the language and  between that and having listened to the  patterns when I speak to my tutor and if  my tutor is patient and asks me  questions we can actually get going in a  conversation with a lot of difficulty so  there you have it  grammar is important it should not be  the focus there should never be an  assumption because a grammar rule is  taught that the student should therefore  be able to reproduce it answered  correctly on tests or quizzes none of  that I don't think I mean if you look at  what happens in Canada where they teach  French in the school system they test  them every year the kids most of them  pass the French test and at the end of  it they can't speak so I'm not a fan of  testing people on grammar at all you  need brief explanations tables and lots  of examples and the understanding is  that the person looks at the grammar  reviews the grammar with not the  intention of learning it or remembering  it but as a part of the process of  becoming aware of how the language works  and that this visiting the grammar rules  and tables and seeing those examples and  then again seeing those same patterns in  meaningful context all is part of that  gradual process of getting used to how  the language works so there you have it  a bit of a discussion on grammar as it  relates to my latest adventure with  Romanian and I will be getting back to  Korean when I come back from Romania  because it is on my list and I  definitely want to achieve a decent  level in Korea so thank you for  listening bye for now
 

